      <h2 style="color: #ffffff; font-size: 1.7rem; font-weight: 800; line-height: 1.35; margin-bottom: 18px;">7‑Layer Protective Packaging System for Fragile Items on Mumbai‑Nagbhid Route</h2>
      <div style="font-size: 1.02rem; line-height: 1.8; color: #cbd5e1;"><p>The <strong>seven‑layer packaging protocol</strong> is a systematic approach used by professional movers to safeguard items during transit, especially when handling delicate goods for a <strong>house shifting service in HSR Layout</strong>. By stacking protective materials in a precise order, the protocol minimizes shock, vibration, and environmental exposure.</p>
<h3>Layer 1 – High‑Density Bubble Cushioning</h3>
<p>High‑density bubble cushioning forms the innermost barrier, conforming tightly around the item’s contours. Its closed‑cell structure absorbs micro‑impacts and prevents direct contact with the outer layers. For example, a crystal glass vase is first wrapped in several sheets of bubble wrap, ensuring that any sudden jolt is diffused before reaching the fragile surface.</p>
<h3>Layer 2 – Multi‑Ply Corrugated Sheets</h3>
<p>Directly surrounding the bubble wrap, multi‑ply corrugated sheets add structural rigidity. The multiple flutes create a sandwich effect that distributes weight evenly across the package. When transporting a bronze temple idol, the corrugated layers act like a protective armor, resisting compression from other boxes stacked above it.</p>
